# Line endings, enforced by git rather than by remembering.
#
# SPEC §7 R57 recorded what happens without this: Python's text-mode write on
# Windows translates \n to \r\n, so a three-line edit came out as a 990-line
# diff and a ten-file change read as 2765 insertions / 2796 deletions instead
# of the real 393 / 415. A diff nobody can review is a change nobody has
# reviewed. Commit b4f897a ("Restore LF line endings across this branch") is
# the cleanup for that; this file is the prevention, and it works regardless
# of which editor, script or agent does the writing.
#
# core.autocrlf is `false` on the machine this was written on, which is the
# right setting -- but it is machine-local and travels with nobody. These
# rules are in the repository, so they apply to every clone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells will not run a batch file with LF endings, so gradlew.bat is
# the one file that must keep CRLF. gradle-wrapper.properties is CRLF today
# only because Gradle wrote it on Windows; it is pinned rather than converted
# so `gradle wrapper` regenerating it does not show up as a whole-file diff.
*.bat            text eol=crlf
gradlew.bat      text eol=crlf
gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties text eol=crlf

# Never touched. The keystore matters most: `text=auto` has to *guess* whether
# a file is text, and a wrong guess here rewrites bytes inside the signing
# identity that both variants share -- which would mean no build could install
# over an existing one without an uninstall, taking the database with it (see
# app/build.gradle.kts's signingConfigs note). Declared explicitly so the
# guess never happens.
*.keystore       binary
*.jks            binary
*.apk            binary
*.aab            binary
*.jar            binary
*.gz             binary
*.zip            binary
*.png            binary
*.jpg            binary
*.webp           binary
*.ttf            binary
*.otf            binary
*.pdf            binary
